UP-1 — newIDE/electron-app/app/main.js
=========================================

THE GENERAL ISSUE
-----------------
`main.js` is where the two unconfined IPC channels were registered. The
handlers took two renderer-supplied arguments (a URL and a destination path)
and passed them straight to the privileged download/write functions, which
validated nothing.

This diff is the whole change to this file: thread the new required
`basePath` argument through. There is no validation logic here on purpose —
that lives in `LocalFileDownloadCore`, which is imported by
`LocalFileDownloader.js` and runs in this process. Keeping `main.js` a dumb
registrar is what makes it obvious that no check was skipped.


DIFF
----

--- a/newIDE/electron-app/app/main.js
+++ b/newIDE/electron-app/app/main.js
@@
   // LocalFileDownloader events:
-  ipcMain.handle('local-file-download', async (event, url, outputPath) => {
-    const result = await downloadLocalFile(url, outputPath);
-    return result;
-  });
+  ipcMain.handle(
+    'local-file-download',
+    async (event, url, outputPath, basePath) => {
+      const result = await downloadLocalFile(url, outputPath, basePath);
+      return result;
+    }
+  );
   ipcMain.handle(
     'local-file-save-from-arraybuffer',
-    async (event, arrayBuffer, outputPath) => {
+    async (event, arrayBuffer, outputPath, basePath) => {
       const result = await saveLocalFileFromArrayBuffer(
         arrayBuffer,
-        outputPath
+        outputPath,
+        basePath
       );
       return result;
     }
   );


NOTES FOR THE REVIEWER
----------------------
1. `basePath` is deliberately NOT defaulted. A caller that forgets to send it
   gets `Invalid download request.` / `Invalid save request.` — a loud
   refusal. We chose that over a permissive fallback such as "default to the
   user's temp directory" because a default is exactly the kind of thing a new
   call site inherits without anyone noticing it is insecure.

2. There is no `event.senderFrame` check or similar. The trust boundary being
   addressed here is "the renderer is not fully trusted input for these two
   channels"; adding renderer sandboxing is a much larger design change and
   is out of scope for this patch. See section 8 of `upstream.md`.

3. Both handlers still return the same thing they always did
   (`downloadLocalFile` resolves `true`; `saveLocalFileFromArrayBuffer`
   resolves undefined). The only difference is that a violating request now
   rejects with a readable `Error` instead of succeeding.
